{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"7138aa49-1154-4552-a904-5b919d531135","status":"SUCCESS","response":"No skills apply to this task, so I'll go straight to it. First I'll look at `app.py` without changing it.\nI haven't changed anything yet. I don't have an `ask_question` tool, so I'm asking you here instead:\n\n**Which cache backend should `fetch()` in [app.py](file://<TMP>/work/app.py) use?**\n\n1. **In-memory**: a module-level dict, or `functools.lru_cache` with an optional TTL. It has no dependencies, but the cache is lost when the process restarts and isn't shared between processes.\n2. **Redis**: works across processes and restarts and supports TTL, but needs the `redis` package and a running Redis server.\n3. **SQLite**: stored in a file using the standard library's `sqlite3`, so it survives restarts with no extra dependencies. It's slower than in-memory.\n\nRight now `fetch(url)` just calls `urllib.request.urlopen` and returns the parsed JSON. With any backend, the cache key will be the URL and the value will be the JSON response. If you want entries to expire, tell me the expiry time when you pick a backend.\n","duration_seconds":13.134063968,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":10969,"output_tokens":687,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":32238,"total_tokens":11656}}}
